---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TestLab from '../components/TestLab.astro';
import ArticleCard from '../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import { getWriting } from '../lib/writing';
const entries = (await getWriting())
  .filter((entry) => entry.data.series === 'Evaluating AI in practice')
  .sort((a, b) => (a.data.order || 0) - (b.data.order || 0));
---

<Base
  title="AI education"
  description="Learn to evaluate AI through concrete examples, research, and engineering practice. Start with a hands-on test quality exercise."
>
  <div class="wrapper">
    <header class="page-header">
      <h1>Build understanding.<br />Then put it to the test.</h1><p>
        AI education for technical readers. Connecting research ideas to examples you can inspect,
        question, and use in engineering practice.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section class="section learning-grid" aria-labelledby="exercise">
      <div>
        <span class="topic-label">Start with an experiment</span><h2 id="exercise">
          A passing test suite.<br />An incorrect function.
        </h2><div class="prose mt">
          <p>
            This Python function is meant to return the largest value in a non-empty list of
            integers. Two tests pass. Before adding a third, predict an input that could expose a
            bug.
          </p><p>
            <strong>You will practice:</strong> identifying an untested input class and distinguishing
            test success from a correctness claim.
          </p><p class="muted">
            Prerequisite: basic Python. This is a deterministic teaching example, not a live AI
            model or a reproduction of a research benchmark.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <TestLab />
    </section>
    <section class="section learning-grid" aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <div><h2 id="lesson">What changed<br />when the test changed?</h2></div><div>
        <ol class="learning-steps">
          <li>
            <strong>State the contract.</strong><p>
              The answer must be an element of the non-empty input list and must be at least as
              large as every other element.
            </p>
          </li><li>
            <strong>Challenge the assumption.</strong><p>
              Initializing the result to zero works on these positive examples. It fails when every
              input is negative.
            </p>
          </li><li>
            <strong>Improve the implementation and the tests.</strong><p>
              Initialize from the first element, then test negative values, duplicates, zero, and
              single-element lists. Define empty-input behavior explicitly.
            </p>
          </li><li>
            <strong>Keep the conclusion proportional.</strong><p>
              Broader tests provide stronger evidence. They do not establish that every possible
              behavior of a complex AI system is correct.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol><details>
          <summary>See a corrected implementation</summary
          ><pre><code>{`def largest(numbers):
    if not numbers:
        raise ValueError("expected a non-empty list")
    best = numbers[0]
    for n in numbers[1:]:
        best = max(best, n)
    return best`}</code></pre><p
          >
            This contract is for lists of integers. Other input types need their own specification.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="reading">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <h2 id="reading">From example to research</h2><a href="/research/">Explore the research</a>
      </div><div class="prose">
        <p>
          The same question becomes more consequential when tests are used to score coding agents.
          UTBoost studies rigorous evaluation on SWE-Bench; SWE-ABS examines inflated success rates
          on test-based benchmarks.
        </p><div class="resource-links">
          <a href="/publications/#yu2025utboost">UTBoost</a><a href="/publications/#yu2026sweabs"
            >SWE-ABS</a
          ><a href="/publications/#yu2023Retro">Retromorphic Testing</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    {
      entries.length > 0 && (
        <section class="section" aria-labelledby="series">
          <>
            <div class="section-heading">
              <h2 id="series">Evaluating AI in practice</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="section-intro">
              Read in order: begin with the evidence a test provides, then examine the benchmark
              built around it.
            </p>
            <div class="article-list">
              {entries.map((entry) => (
                <ArticleCard entry={entry} />
              ))}
            </div>
          </>
        </section>
      )
    }
  </div>
</Base>
